<article class="env-lemma" id="02IT">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02IT">Lemma <span data-tag="02IT">28.9.2</span>.</a> Let $X$ be a scheme. The following are equivalent: </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$X$ is regular, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$X$ is locally Noetherian and all of its local rings are regular, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$X$ is locally Noetherian and for any closed point $x \in X$ the local ring $\mathcal{O}_{X, x}$ is regular. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      By the discussion in Algebra preceding Algebra, Definition <a href="/tag/00OD" data-tag="00OD">10.110.7</a> we know that the localization of a regular local ring is regular. The lemma follows by combining this with Lemma <a href="/tag/01OW" data-tag="01OW">28.5.2</a>, with the existence of closed points on locally Noetherian schemes (Lemma <a href="/tag/02IL" data-tag="02IL">28.5.9</a>), and the definitions. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>